1. the red outline was too red, it looses its depth. 
2. some of the items doesn't place on the ground it places halfway in the ground
3. the items can be placed on walls, lets add a type on the item name so we can see if it can be placed on the walls layer or not. In addition if its placed on walls the gravity and outside movement should be turned off. 
4. items can be placed through things, lets not allow placement if it can't fit. 
5. lets only do a red outline if it can't fit otherwise it just shows the transparent item.  

1. Fix the “too red, no depth” issue

Instead of tinting materials red/green (which kills shading), do a depth-preserving transparent tint.

Replace your color code with this:

private void ApplyGhostTint(Renderer r, bool valid)
{
    foreach (var mat in r.materials)
    {
        Color tint = valid ? new Color(1, 1, 1, 0.35f) : new Color(1, 0.3f, 0.3f, 0.45f);
        mat.color = tint;
        mat.SetFloat("_Surface", 1);      // transparent
        mat.SetFloat("_ZWrite", 0);       // keep depth
        mat.EnableKeyword("_SURFACE_TYPE_TRANSPARENT");
    }
}


Then call it from a new unified method:

private void UpdateGhostColor(GameObject ghost, bool valid)
{
    foreach (var r in ghost.GetComponentsInChildren<Renderer>())
        ApplyGhostTint(r, valid);
}


This gives:

Color visible
Depth preserved
Ghost still looks 3D

2. Fix items spawning halfway inside ground

Your code currently sets:

targetPos = hit.point;


But if the object's pivot is not at the bottom, it sinks.

Fix: Offset by collider half-height

Add:

private Vector3 GetBottomAlignedPosition(GameObject ghost, Vector3 hitPoint)
{
    if (ghost.TryGetComponent(out Collider col))
        return hitPoint + Vector3.up * (col.bounds.extents.y * 0.95f);

    return hitPoint;
}


Then update placement:

targetPos = GetBottomAlignedPosition(ghostPreview, hit.point);


This ensures:

Object sits ON the ground
Zero sinking
Works with any pivot position

3. Placement type: floor-only, wall-only, or both

Add this enum:

public enum PlacementType { Floor, Wall, Both }


Your item definition:

public class ItemDefinition : ScriptableObject
{
    public GameObject ghostPrefab;
    public PlacementType placementType;
    public bool disablePhysicsWhenPlaced; // for wall objects
}


When placing, detect the surface:

LayerMask ground = LayerMask.GetMask("Ground");
LayerMask wall   = LayerMask.GetMask("Wall");


Add validation:

private bool ValidateSurface(ItemDefinition def, RaycastHit hit)
{
    int layer = hit.collider.gameObject.layer;

    if (layer == LayerMask.NameToLayer("Ground") && 
        (def.placementType == PlacementType.Floor || def.placementType == PlacementType.Both))
        return true;

    if (layer == LayerMask.NameToLayer("Wall") &&
        (def.placementType == PlacementType.Wall || def.placementType == PlacementType.Both))
        return true;

    return false;
}


During placement raycast:

bool surfaceAllowed = ValidateSurface(def, hit);

if (!surfaceAllowed)
{
    UpdateGhostColor(ghostPreview, false);
    return;
}

4. Prevent “through wall” placement (true fit-test)

Your current OverlapBox uses bounds (which is good), but you forgot to disable your ghost collider AFTER testing, so it interferes.

Fix:

(A) Disable collider before test — already done, good
(B) Expand overlap slightly
(C) Ignore triggers & walls if disallowed

Updated check:

private bool IsPlacementValid(GameObject ghost)
{
    Collider col = ghost.GetComponentInChildren<Collider>();
    if (!col) return true;

    Vector3 pos = ghost.transform.position;
    Quaternion rot = ghost.transform.rotation;
    Vector3 size = col.bounds.size * 0.95f; // more strict and prevents clipping

    // Don't collide with the ground layer; avoid self-intersection
    LayerMask mask = ~LayerMask.GetMask("Ground", "Ghost");

    Collider[] hits = Physics.OverlapBox(pos, size / 2f, rot, mask, QueryTriggerInteraction.Ignore);

    if (hits.Length > 0)
        return false;

    // Ground check remains
    if (!Physics.Raycast(pos + Vector3.up * 0.2f, Vector3.down, 2f, LayerMask.GetMask("Ground")))
        return false;

    return true;
}


Now objects cannot be placed inside other objects.

5. Only show red outline when invalid

Right now you always tint the ghost.

Fix: Only color red if invalid
Otherwise use a neutral transparent color

Update your tint method:

private void UpdateGhostColor(GameObject ghost, bool valid)
{
    foreach (var r in ghost.GetComponentsInChildren<Renderer>())
    {
        Color tint = valid ? new Color(1, 1, 1, 0.35f) 
                           : new Color(1, 0.2f, 0.2f, 0.45f);

        foreach (var mat in r.materials)
            mat.color = tint;
    }
}


Now:

Default ghost is transparent white
Invalid = transparent red

